# Procurement Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## Article I — Purpose

This policy establishes standards for the procurement of goods and services by {{ org_name }} ("the Organization") using federal and non-federal funds. It is designed to ensure that materials and services are obtained efficiently, at reasonable cost, and in compliance with the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.318–327) and applicable state law.

This policy applies to all purchases regardless of funding source, so that the Organization maintains consistent procurement practices across all programs.

## Article II — Scope

This policy applies to all directors, officers, employees, and agents of the Organization who are authorized to commit organizational funds for the purchase of goods, services, equipment, or supplies.

## Article III — General Procurement Standards

### 3.1 Oversight and Responsibility

The Executive Director (or their designee) is responsible for ensuring that all procurement transactions comply with this policy. The Board of Directors shall exercise oversight through regular financial reporting and the annual audit process.

### 3.2 Written Procedures

All procurement actions above the micro-purchase threshold must be documented in writing, including the rationale for the method of procurement, the basis for contractor selection, and the basis for the contract price.

### 3.3 Necessary and Reasonable Purchases

The Organization shall avoid acquiring unnecessary items. Where appropriate, an analysis shall be made of lease-versus-purchase alternatives and consideration given to consolidating or breaking out procurements to obtain a more economical purchase.

### 3.4 Open Competition

All procurement transactions shall be conducted in a manner that provides full and open competition consistent with the standards of 2 CFR 200.319. The Organization shall not place unreasonable requirements on vendors, require unnecessary experience or excessive bonding, or engage in organizational conflicts of interest.

### 3.5 Vendor Verification

The Organization shall verify that vendors are not suspended or debarred from receiving federal funds by checking the System for Award Management (SAM.gov) before awarding any contract funded in whole or in part by federal awards.

## Article IV — Methods of Procurement

### 4.1 Micro-Purchases (up to $10,000)

Purchases where the aggregate dollar amount does not exceed **$10,000** (the federal micro-purchase threshold per 2 CFR 200.320(a)(1)) may be awarded without soliciting competitive quotations, provided:

- The price is considered reasonable based on research, experience, purchase history, or other relevant information;
- Purchases are distributed equitably among qualified suppliers to the extent practicable; and
- No splitting of purchases is used to avoid a higher procurement threshold.

A single approver (the Executive Director or designee) may authorize micro-purchases.

### 4.2 Small Purchases ($10,001–$250,000)

For purchases exceeding the micro-purchase threshold but not exceeding the **Simplified Acquisition Threshold of $250,000**, the Organization shall obtain price or rate quotations from an adequate number of qualified sources (a minimum of **three** quotations is expected where possible).

The purchase shall be awarded to the vendor offering the best value, considering price, quality, timeliness, and past performance.

### 4.3 Sealed Bids (Formal Advertising)

Sealed bidding is appropriate when:

- A complete, adequate, and realistic specification or purchase description is available;
- Two or more responsible bidders are willing and able to compete;
- The procurement lends itself to a firm fixed-price contract; and
- Selection can be made principally on the basis of price.

When sealed bidding is used, the Organization shall publicly solicit bids, open them at a stated time and place, and award a firm fixed-price contract to the lowest responsive and responsible bidder. Any or all bids may be rejected if there is a sound documented reason.

### 4.4 Competitive Proposals

When sealed bidding is not appropriate (e.g., for professional services, research subcontracts, or complex procurements), the Organization shall solicit proposals from an adequate number of qualified sources and evaluate them against published evaluation criteria. The contract shall be awarded to the responsible firm whose proposal is most advantageous to the Organization, with price and other factors considered.

For procurements exceeding $250,000, the Organization must conduct a cost or price analysis and negotiate profit as a separate element of the price.

### 4.5 Noncompetitive Proposals (Sole Source)

Procurement by noncompetitive proposals (sole source) may be used **only** when one or more of the following circumstances apply (per 2 CFR 200.320(c)):

1. The item or service is available only from a single source;
2. A public exigency or emergency will not permit the delay from competitive solicitation;
3. The federal awarding agency or pass-through entity expressly authorizes noncompetitive procurement in response to a written request; or
4. After solicitation from a number of sources, competition is determined to be inadequate.

**Sole source justification must be documented in writing** and approved by the Executive Director. The justification must include:

- A description of the goods or services;
- An explanation of why competition is not feasible;
- The basis for the contract price; and
- A description of efforts made to identify alternative sources.

For sole source procurements exceeding $250,000 on federally funded projects, prior written approval from the federal awarding agency is required.

## Article V — Conflict of Interest in Procurement

### 5.1 Standards of Conduct

No employee, officer, or agent of the Organization shall participate in the selection, award, or administration of a contract if a real or apparent conflict of interest would be involved (per 2 CFR 200.318(c)(1)). A conflict arises when any of the following has a financial or other interest in the firm selected for an award:

- The employee, officer, or agent;
- Any member of their immediate family;
- Their domestic partner; or
- An organization that employs or is about to employ any of the above.

### 5.2 Gifts and Gratuities

Officers, employees, and agents of the Organization shall neither solicit nor accept gratuities, favors, or anything of monetary value from contractors or parties to subcontracts, except where the gift is an unsolicited item of nominal value (under $25). Violations may result in disciplinary action up to and including termination.

### 5.3 Disclosure

Any individual involved in a procurement action who becomes aware of a potential conflict of interest must disclose it immediately to the Executive Director (or to the Board Chair if the Executive Director has the conflict). The conflicted individual shall be excluded from the procurement decision.

## Article VI — Contract Administration

### 6.1 Contract Oversight

The Organization shall maintain a system of contract administration to ensure that contractors perform in accordance with the terms, conditions, and specifications of their contracts or purchase orders. The Executive Director (or designee) is responsible for monitoring contractor performance.

### 6.2 Required Contract Provisions

All contracts and purchase orders funded by federal awards shall include the applicable provisions described in Appendix II to 2 CFR Part 200, including but not limited to:

- Equal Employment Opportunity (contracts over $10,000);
- Davis-Bacon Act provisions (where applicable to construction);
- Rights to inventions (for research contracts);
- Clean Air Act and Federal Water Pollution Control Act (contracts over $150,000);
- Byrd Anti-Lobbying Amendment (contracts over $100,000);
- Debarment and suspension verification; and
- Access to records by the federal awarding agency, Comptroller General, and the Organization.

### 6.3 Contract Modifications

Any material change to a contract (scope, price, or timeline) must be documented in a written amendment and approved at the same authority level as the original procurement.

## Article VII — Documentation and Records

### 7.1 Procurement Records

The Organization shall maintain records sufficient to detail the history of each procurement, including but not limited to:

- Rationale for the method of procurement;
- Selection of contract type;
- Contractor selection or rejection criteria and justification;
- Quotations, bids, or proposals received;
- The basis for the contract price; and
- Verification of vendor eligibility (SAM.gov check).

### 7.2 Retention Period

Procurement records shall be retained for a minimum of **three years** from the date of submission of the final expenditure report for the award, or three years from the date of the transaction for non-federal purchases, consistent with the Organization's Document Retention Policy and 2 CFR 200.334.

## Article VIII — Domestic Preferences

In accordance with 2 CFR 200.322, the Organization shall, to the greatest extent practicable, provide a preference for the purchase, acquisition, or use of goods, products, or materials produced in the United States. This includes, but is not limited to, iron, aluminum, steel, cement, and other manufactured products.

When purchasing items where domestic alternatives are available at a reasonable cost premium (generally not exceeding 10% above the lowest foreign-sourced price), the domestic product should be preferred. Exceptions must be documented.

## Article IX — Approval Authority

| Procurement Amount        | Required Approval               |
|---------------------------|---------------------------------|
| Up to $10,000             | Executive Director or designee  |
| $10,001–$50,000           | Executive Director              |
| $50,001–$250,000          | Executive Director + Board Chair |
| Over $250,000             | Board of Directors (majority vote) |

Splitting purchases to circumvent these thresholds is prohibited.

## Article X — Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy annually. The Executive Director shall report to the Board on procurement activity, including any sole source justifications and any instances of noncompliance. This review may take place at any regular or special board meeting and shall be noted in the meeting minutes.
